<%# The red surround an admin sees while looking at the app as somebody else.

    It is drawn as one fixed box over the viewport rather than as a wrapper
    around the page, because it has to behave the same in all five layouts and
    two of them (`modal` and `application`) hang a full-height flex column off
    `body` that a wrapper div would break. Fixed also means it cannot be
    scrolled away: on a long page a border on the document would leave the top
    edge somewhere above the fold, and a warning you have to scroll to find is
    not a warning.

    The frame itself takes no clicks (`pointer-events-none`), so the 6px it
    covers on each edge costs nothing; the bar inside it takes them back. The
    page is pushed clear of the bar by a rule on `body:has(> .impersonation-frame)`
    in the stylesheet, which is why this partial is rendered as a direct child
    of `<body>` in every layout.

    It names signed_in_user and not current_user on purpose: if the account
    being viewed is a parent who has stepped into one of their children, both
    strips are on screen, and this one answers "whose account is this" while
    the child bar below answers "whose profile is open inside it". %>
<div class="impersonation-frame">
  <div class="impersonation-bar" role="region" aria-label="<%= t("impersonation.region_label") %>">
    <span aria-hidden="true">👁</span>

    <span class="impersonation-who">
      <%= t("impersonation.viewing_as") %>
      <strong><%= signed_in_user.name %></strong>
      <span class="impersonation-role"><%= t("overseer.users.roles.#{signed_in_user.role}") %></span>
    </span>

    <span class="impersonation-note"><%= t("impersonation.read_only") %></span>

    <%= button_to t("impersonation.stop"), stop_impersonating_path, method: :delete,
          form_class: "ml-auto shrink-0", class: "impersonation-stop" %>
  </div>
</div>
